WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:08.203
Scientists have described about two million species, and there are likely millions more still waiting to be discovered.

00:00:08.203 --> 00:00:14.209
To study that much life, biologists need a way to name every organism and sort it into groups.

00:00:14.209 --> 00:00:16.190
That system is called taxonomy.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:23.200
Taxonomy is the branch of biology that names, describes, and classifies living things.

00:00:23.917 --> 00:00:26.459
Modern taxonomy is built on evolution.

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:36.762
Biologists group organisms by how closely related they are, and they show those relationships with a phylogenetic tree, sometimes called the tree of life.

00:00:37.462 --> 00:00:41.408
Each branch point on the tree represents a common ancestor.

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:46.491
Organisms that share a more recent common ancestor are more closely related.

00:00:46.491 --> 00:00:52.177
So on this tree, a cat is more closely related to a dog than either one is to a frog.

00:00:53.428 --> 00:00:58.701
The system we use today started with Carl Linnaeus, a Swedish botanist.

00:00:58.701 --> 00:01:06.498
In 1735 he published a book called Systema Naturae, which tried to organize every known plant and animal.

00:01:07.198 --> 00:01:13.102
Linnaeus sorted organisms by their morphology, meaning their physical form and structure.

00:01:13.102 --> 00:01:16.618
Organisms that looked alike went into the same group.

00:01:17.868 --> 00:01:23.293
But looks can be misleading, so biologists have to ask why two organisms share a trait.

00:01:24.002 --> 00:01:29.108
Homologous traits are similar because they were inherited from a common ancestor.

00:01:29.108 --> 00:01:35.980
A human arm, a bat wing, and a whale flipper do different jobs, but they're built from the same set of bones.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.817
That shared plan points to a shared ancestor.

00:01:39.517 --> 00:01:45.290
Analogous traits are similar because they do the same job, not because of shared ancestry.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:52.860
Bird wings and insect wings both allow flight, but they evolved separately and are built in completely different ways.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.607
Analogous traits can trick you into grouping unrelated organisms together.

00:01:58.317 --> 00:02:06.667
Today, taxonomists combine physical traits with DNA evidence to figure out which similarities really come from a common ancestor.

00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:11.196
Linnaeus also gave us a ranked system of groups.

00:02:11.196 --> 00:02:15.637
Each group at any rank is called a taxon, and the plural is taxa.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:23.487
From broadest to most specific, the ranks are domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, and species.

00:02:23.487 --> 00:02:25.207
To remember the order, try:

00:02:25.207 --> 00:02:27.820
Dear King Philip Came Over For Good Soup.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.924
Here's how the house cat is classified.

00:02:30.924 --> 00:02:36.840
As you move down the ranks, each group gets smaller, and its members have more traits in common.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:41.482
Every species gets a two-part scientific name.

00:02:41.482 --> 00:02:44.210
This is called binomial nomenclature.

00:02:44.923 --> 00:02:52.479
The first word is the genus, and the second is the specific epithet, which identifies the species within that genus.

00:02:52.479 --> 00:02:54.368
The house cat is Felis catus.

00:02:55.068 --> 00:02:55.697
The rules:

00:02:55.697 --> 00:03:04.368
the genus is capitalized, the second word is lowercase, and the whole name is written in italics, or underlined when you write it by hand.

00:03:05.068 --> 00:03:05.843
Why bother?

00:03:05.843 --> 00:03:08.659
Common names change from place to place.

00:03:08.659 --> 00:03:14.784
One big cat is called a cougar, a puma, or a mountain lion, depending on where you are.

00:03:14.784 --> 00:03:19.853
Its scientific name, Puma concolor, is the same everywhere in the world.

00:03:21.103 --> 00:03:24.053
At the very top of the system are three domains.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:29.727
Bacteria are single-celled prokaryotes, meaning their cells have no nucleus.

00:03:29.727 --> 00:03:33.640
They live almost everywhere, including inside your own body.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:42.695
Archaea are also single-celled prokaryotes, but their chemistry is different enough from bacteria that they get their own domain.

00:03:42.695 --> 00:03:47.035
Many live in extreme places, like hot springs and very salty water.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:53.770
Eukarya includes every organism whose cells have a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:57.835
That means you, along with every plant, fungus, and protist.

00:03:59.085 --> 00:04:02.436
The domain Eukarya is split into four kingdoms.

00:04:02.436 --> 00:04:06.145
A useful way to compare them is how they get energy.

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:10.280
Autotrophs make their own food, usually by photosynthesis.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.345
Heterotrophs have to get their food from other organisms.

00:04:15.052 --> 00:04:19.992
Protista is the catch-all kingdom for eukaryotes that don't fit anywhere else.

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:25.122
Most are single-celled, like amoebas, and they can be autotrophs or heterotrophs.

00:04:25.822 --> 00:04:29.165
Fungi, like mushrooms and yeasts, are heterotrophs.

00:04:29.165 --> 00:04:33.492
They release enzymes and absorb nutrients from their surroundings.

00:04:34.192 --> 00:04:39.842
Plantae are multicellular autotrophs that make their own food through photosynthesis.

00:04:40.558 --> 00:04:48.508
Animalia are multicellular heterotrophs that eat other organisms, and most of them can move around at some point in their lives.

00:04:49.758 --> 00:04:50.696
Let's review.

00:04:50.696 --> 00:04:59.353
Taxonomy names and classifies living things based on evolutionary relationships, which are shown on a phylogenetic tree.

00:05:00.058 --> 00:05:05.758
Homologous traits come from a common ancestor, while analogous traits just do the same job.

00:05:06.458 --> 00:05:11.783
Organisms are sorted into eight ranks, from domain all the way down to species.

00:05:12.492 --> 00:05:16.917
Every species has a two-part scientific name, like Felis catus.

00:05:17.625 --> 00:05:20.486
And all of life falls into three domains:

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:22.650
Bacteria, Archaea, and Eukarya.
